# tests/T11.bd - T11: THE LETHAL BURST BOUNDARY.
# Second test of the KILL-TRIGGER block (T10 was the first).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE QUESTION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   What is the SMALLEST unpaced burst that kills - for pure arithmetic, for
#   the cheapest log line, and for a log line with one extra concatenation -
#   is that boundary SHARP or STOCHASTIC, and does inserting get_cpu() calls
#   into the burst move it?
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS IS THE MOST IMPORTANT TEST IN THE PROGRAMME
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Everything the corpus knows about the lethal burst size is two loose
#   anecdotes from single uninstrumented runs: "50 plain arithmetic lines"
#   (test_cpu_decay.bd) and "~13 unpaced log() iterations" (field_notes.txt).
#   tests/README.txt bounds it only as "at most 50, likely well under". It has
#   NEVER been bisected, and - far more important - NOBODY HAS EVER RUN THE
#   SAME BURST SIZE TWICE. So nobody knows whether the kill line is a fixed
#   number at all.
#
#   That second half is the whole ballgame. The central unexplained finding of
#   this investigation is that identical runs of identical code over identical
#   data died at +40.7s, +111.0s, +40.3s, +65.5s and +64.3s. Every hypothesis
#   tried so far has looked for the variance inside menu.bd, and every one has
#   failed. If a fixed burst of n lines sometimes survives and sometimes dies,
#   the variance is in the ENGINE'S KILL TRIGGER and menu.bd was never the
#   subject. That single binary would explain the entire 2.7x spread, and this
#   script is the cheapest possible way to ask it.
#
#   Note also what T10 and test_decode_pacing already imply: PART A of
#   test_decode_pacing sat at a LOGGED cpu=100 for ~840 real seconds and never
#   died. So the killer is NOT integrating get_cpu()'s own output - whatever it
#   counts, the meter is not it. That is exactly why this test is meter-free:
#   the observable is kill / no-kill, a binary the meter cannot perturb, alias
#   or smooth.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BURST IS THE EXPERIMENT (deliberate, not an oversight)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Like T10, this script is allowed to violate the "sleep() every 5-8 lines"
#   rule INSIDE the burst functions. Measuring an unpaced burst is the point.
#   Everything outside the burst is paced normally: every burst has sleep(S)
#   immediately before it (the burst function's own first line) and sleep(S)
#   immediately after it, so no announcement line can ever be mistaken for the
#   thing that did the killing.
#
#   Every burst function's FIRST line is its own sleep(s). That is deliberate:
#   it means the mode-dispatch if/elif chain, the RUNG announcement and the
#   loop bookkeeping all land on the far side of a sleep from the burst, so
#   the differing chain depth per arm (1 test for arith, 6 for concatlog)
#   cannot leak into the measured window. Inside the window every arm is
#   identical except for its loop body. A mistyped mode falls through to
#   arith while the header echoes the string you typed, so a typo shows up as
#   a mode= label that does not match the output shape.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SIX ARMS - one harness, five orthogonal deltas
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Every arm has an IDENTICAL 4-line preamble (t, j, x, c) inside the unpaced
#   window and an identical `return` line closing it, so that constant offset
#   cancels exactly out of every comparison below. In the log arms x and c are
#   unused on purpose - they are structural padding, not dead code.
#
#     arm        loop body                             lines  extra ops
#     arith      x = x + 1 / j = j + 1                   2     none
#     zero       x = x + 1 / c = 0        / j = j + 1    3     none
#     cpu        x = x + 1 / c = get_cpu()/ j = j + 1    3     get_cpu()
#     time       x = x + 1 / c = get_unix_time()/j=j+1   3     get_unix_time()
#     intlog     j = j + 1 / log(str(j))                 2     str() + log()
#     concatlog  j = j + 1 / log("x" + str(j))           2     str()+ "+" +log()
#
#   The five deltas those arms buy:
#     arith  vs zero       +1 LINE, no new operation  -> price of a bare line
#     zero   vs cpu        same lines, +get_cpu()     -> THE OBSERVER EFFECT
#     zero   vs time       same lines, +get_unix_time() -> "any call" control
#     arith  vs intlog     same lines, log()+str()    -> price of logging
#     intlog vs concatlog  same lines, +1 concat      -> price of ONE operation
#
#   zero/cpu/time are the hard three: same line count, same loop shape, same
#   call depth, differing ONLY in the right-hand side of one assignment. If
#   cpu's boundary drops and time's does not, the surcharge belongs to
#   get_cpu() specifically. If both drop equally, it belongs to "making a
#   call". If neither drops, calls are free in kill currency and T05's
#   wall-clock verdict has to be reconciled with that.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW THE HARNESS WORKS - one loop, two experiments, chosen by `step`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   step > 0  LADDER MODE. Rungs of n, n+step, n+2*step ... each separated by
#             2*S seconds of sleep. Finds the boundary IN ONE RUN instead of
#             one run per candidate value. The last "RUNG" line with no "OK"
#             line after it names the size that killed it; the last "OK" line
#             names the largest survivor.
#   step = 0  HOLD MODE. The SAME n, over and over, `rungs` times, in one run.
#             This is the sharpness test, and it is why this script exists.
#             If rungs 0,1,2 print OK and rung 3 does not, the same burst size
#             both survived and died WITHIN ONE RUN - the headline result.
#   rungs = 1 SINGLE SHOT. One burst at exactly n. Used for fresh-run repeats,
#             which is the sharpness test done the slow, unimpeachable way
#             (see KNOWN CONFOUND below for why both forms are needed).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CALIB LINE - the script states its own prediction before testing it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Before the ladder starts, every run times one fixed 8-iteration arith
#   burst (24-28 ticks: comfortably under T02's proven-survivable 30-line
#   window, and it never changes, in any arm, in any run - so it biases
#   nothing that is compared against anything else).
#     CALIB arith8=<cal>s pred_n=<p>
#   cal / 8 is seconds per arith iteration on THIS machine's actual tier.
#   pred_n = round(40 / cal) is what the documented rule - "100% CPU for 5
#   straight seconds kills" - predicts the lethal arith n must be. The script
#   prints its own falsifiable prediction, in-band, before producing the
#   number that confirms or refutes it. At base tier expect cal ~2.8s and
#   pred_n ~14.
#   If pred_n comes back far from the ladder you ran, re-run the ladder
#   centred on pred_n. If cal prints as a whole number or 0, get_unix_time()
#   is 1-second-resolution on this build (see T01/T02) - pred_n is then coarse
#   but still usable; ignore any burst= value under ~2s.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OUTPUT FORMAT - every line is a complete datum on its own
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   KILLBOUND mode=<m> n=<N> step=<S> rungs=<R> settle=<s> entry_cpu=<c>
#       Printed before anything else runs. entry_cpu is read on the script's
#       VERY FIRST executable line, above even the function definitions, so it
#       is a true carryover probe: ~0 on a cold run (test_cpu_decay saw exactly
#       0), materially above 0 on a run started seconds after a kill. It is the
#       ONLY get_cpu() call anywhere in this script outside the cpu arm itself.
#   CALIB arith8=<cal>s pred_n=<p>                    see above
#   RUNG r=<r> mode=<m> n=<k>    printed BEFORE the burst. If this is the last
#       line in the paste, burst size k is what killed the script. This line
#       alone is a complete result.
#   OK r=<r> n=<k> burst=<el>s   printed only AFTER the post-burst sleep, so it
#       proves the burst itself survived. burst= is the meter-free ruler:
#       el / k = seconds per iteration, and el at the boundary converts the
#       kill line into SECONDS, which is what tests the documented 5s rule.
#   DONE mode=<m> top_n=<k>      nothing died at any rung. Raise n/step, re-run.
#   In the intlog/concatlog arms the burst prints a rising integer (bare `13`)
#   or a prefixed one (`x13`) per iteration - so those arms SELF-REPORT: the
#   largest number printed before the overload banner IS the boundary, no
#   sweep needed, and the bare-vs-prefixed format tells the two arms apart at
#   a glance in a paste.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS - written before any run, base tier assumed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   P1 SHARP: some n survives all trials and n+step dies on all trials.
#   P2 n*_arith lands within +/-2 rungs of the CALIB line's pred_n, and the
#      burst= at the largest survivor is 4.0-5.5s. (Base tier: n* ~15 if the
#      `while` condition costs a tick, ~23 if it is free. burst= at n*
#      discriminates the two without needing T02.)
#   P3 PER-LINE CHARGING: n*_zero / n*_arith ~= 2/3 (3 ticks/iter vs 2), i.e.
#      the two boundaries COINCIDE IN SECONDS even though they differ in n.
#   P4 OBSERVER EFFECT: n*_cpu < n*_zero if get_cpu() is not free. One extra
#      tick per call predicts n*_cpu ~= (3/4) * n*_zero.
#   P5 SPECIFICITY: n*_time == n*_zero. get_unix_time() is the confirmed-free
#      control; if it moves too, the cost is "a call", not "the meter".
#   P6 n*_intlog ~= n*_arith under per-line charging; below it if log() carries
#      a per-operation surcharge. The anecdote ("~13 log iterations") predicts
#      roughly the same neighbourhood, which is itself suspicious and worth
#      pinning down.
#   P7 n*_concatlog == n*_intlog under per-line charging; strictly lower under
#      per-operation charging.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   SHARP AND REPEATABLE (P1 holds, every arm)
#       The kill is a deterministic count of consecutive unpaced ticks.
#       Convert n* to seconds via burst=; if it lands on ~5.0s the documented
#       rule is literally implemented and the load model is finally settled.
#       The run-to-run variance then does NOT live in the kill trigger and the
#       search goes back upstream - but now with a calibrated budget: any
#       stretch of menu.bd longer than n* consecutive lines is a live bomb,
#       and that is directly actionable.
#   NOT SHARP (same n both survives and dies)
#       THE HEADLINE. The kill line is phase-dependent - where the burst
#       starts relative to the engine's own tick/second boundary decides
#       whether it dies. That is a complete, sufficient mechanism for
#       +40.7 / +111.0 / +40.3 / +65.5 / +64.3s, and it means no amount of
#       pacing work on menu.bd could ever have produced a stable result.
#       Everything downstream (T12's 70%-of-lethal bursts, T13) has to be
#       re-cut around a probabilistic threshold instead of a fixed one.
#   n*_cpu < n*_zero, n*_time == n*_zero
#       get_cpu() adds real load, proven WITHOUT ever trusting get_cpu()'s own
#       output. field_notes' unquantified CORRECTION is upheld and now has a
#       magnitude. throttle()'s `while get_cpu() > t: sleep(1)` is provably
#       feeding the meter it is waiting on.
#   n*_cpu == n*_zero == n*_time
#       get_cpu() is free in kill currency. If T05's wall clock said otherwise,
#       the two currencies disagree and THAT disagreement is the result: the
#       thing the killer counts is not the thing that costs wall-clock time.
#       Do not average them; report both.
#   intlog == concatlog, arith == intlog
#       Charging is strictly per executed LINE. What a line DOES is irrelevant.
#       Pacing rules can be written purely in line counts, which is a large
#       simplification for every script in this project.
#   concatlog < intlog (or intlog < arith)
#       Charging is per OPERATION. Line counting is not a safe budget and
#       every "throttle every N lines" rule in the codebase is mis-calibrated.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KNOWN CONFOUND - ladder carryover, and how the protocol controls it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   A ladder and a hold run put several bursts inside ONE script run. If load
#   does not fully clear across settle seconds, rung 3 starts hotter than rung
#   0 and dies for the wrong reason. That is why STAGE 3 repeats the boundary
#   as FRESH single-shot runs. Compare:
#     ladder n* == fresh-run n*   -> settle is sufficient, no carryover.
#     ladder n* <  fresh-run n*   -> LOAD SURVIVES THE SLEEP. That is not a
#         spoiled experiment, it is a second mechanism for the central
#         mystery, and it is measurable: re-run the ladder with settle=8 and
#         see where the boundary moves. (T08 measured the decay curve from
#         saturation; settle=3 is chosen against it. If T08 showed saturation
#         persisting past 3s, run everything here at settle=8 instead.)
#   Be honest about the ordering: whether a sleep resets the kill timer at all
#   is T12's question, and T12 runs AFTER this one. So the ladder and hold
#   forms rest on an assumption this programme has not yet tested. That is
#   exactly why STAGE 3 exists and why no boundary is reported from a ladder
#   alone. If T12 later shows a sleep does NOT fully reset the timer, every
#   ladder n* here has to be re-read as a lower bound, while the STAGE 3
#   fresh-run numbers stand unaffected.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN - @param binds POSITIONALLY, no -flag= syntax
#   T11 <mode> <n> <step> <rungs> <settle>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Run FIRST THING in a fresh session. Confirm with `ps` and `jobs` that
#   nothing else is running, and paste that too. Paste EVERY line of EVERY
#   run verbatim, including the "[SYSTEM] CPU overloaded..." banner and
#   including runs that printed only a header - those ARE the data. Leave
#   ~20s idle between runs unless a stage says otherwise.
#
#   STAGE 1 - locate the boundary          (1 run, ~45-130s)
#       T11 arith
#     Defaults ladder 6,9,12,...,33. Record pred_n from CALIB, the largest
#     n with an OK line (call it nS) and the smallest n without one (nL).
#     If it prints DONE, re-run as: T11 arith 36 3 10
#
#   STAGE 2 - sharpness, survivor side     (1 run, ~70s)
#       T11 arith <nS> 0 8
#     Eight identical bursts at the largest survivor. ANY missing OK line
#     after an earlier OK line is the headline result.
#
#   STAGE 3 - sharpness, lethal side       (3 runs, ~15s each)
#       T11 arith <nL> 0 1
#     Run it three times, ~20s apart. Any single SURVIVED/OK is the headline
#     result. Three repeats is the minimum that can tell a deterministic
#     boundary from a stochastic one; if ANY flip appears anywhere, go to five.
#     STAGES 1-3 together are under 4 minutes and answer the main question.
#
#   STAGE 4 - the observer arms            (3 runs, ~45-130s each)
#       T11 zero <a> <b> 10
#       T11 cpu  <a> <b> 10
#       T11 time <a> <b> 10
#     Use IDENTICAL <a> and <b> for all three - boundaries measured on
#     different grids are not comparable. Start them ~1/3 lower than arith's
#     ladder, since these arms cost 3 body lines per iteration instead of 2:
#     if arith gave nL=15, use  4 2 10  (4,6,8,...,22).
#
#   STAGE 5 - the log arms                 (2 runs, ~20s each)
#       T11 intlog 200 0 1
#       T11 concatlog 200 0 1
#     These self-report: the last integer printed before the banner IS the
#     boundary. n=200 is just a ceiling it is not expected to reach.
#
#   TOTAL: ~10 runs, ~7 minutes, no single run over ~2.2 minutes, and the
#   protocol yields a usable result if stopped after ANY stage.
#
# NOTE ON try/catch: deliberately not used. If T06 established that the
# overload kill is catchable, this script can be upgraded to report its own
# death and collapse stages 2/3 into one run - but as written it assumes
# nothing about catchability, and a catch that silently swallowed the kill
# would destroy the only observable this test has.
#
# NOTE ON CURRENCY: T14 (charging unit), T15 (loop cost) and T18 (log price)
# ask overlapping per-construct questions in WALL-CLOCK currency, and all run
# after this one. T11 asks them in KILL currency, early and coarsely. The two
# currencies are not guaranteed to agree - test_decode_pacing sat at a logged
# cpu=100 for ~840s without dying, which already shows the killer counts
# something other than the meter. Where T11 and T14/T15/T18 disagree, report
# both and treat the disagreement as the finding; do not average them.
# @param mode "arith | zero | cpu | time | intlog | concatlog" default="arith"
# @param n "first rung size, in loop iterations" default="6"
# @param step "rung increment; 0 = repeat the same n (sharpness test)" default="3"
# @param rungs "how many rungs before giving up" default="10"
# @param settle "seconds of sleep before AND after every burst" default="3"

var c0 = get_cpu()

func b_arith(n, s):
    sleep(s)
    var t = get_unix_time()
    var j = 0
    var x = 0
    var c = 0
    while j < n:
        x = x + 1
        j = j + 1
    return get_unix_time() - t

sleep(1)

func b_zero(n, s):
    sleep(s)
    var t = get_unix_time()
    var j = 0
    var x = 0
    var c = 0
    while j < n:
        x = x + 1
        c = 0
        j = j + 1
    return get_unix_time() - t

sleep(1)

func b_cpu(n, s):
    sleep(s)
    var t = get_unix_time()
    var j = 0
    var x = 0
    var c = 0
    while j < n:
        x = x + 1
        c = get_cpu()
        j = j + 1
    return get_unix_time() - t

sleep(1)

func b_time(n, s):
    sleep(s)
    var t = get_unix_time()
    var j = 0
    var x = 0
    var c = 0
    while j < n:
        x = x + 1
        c = get_unix_time()
        j = j + 1
    return get_unix_time() - t

sleep(1)

func b_intlog(n, s):
    sleep(s)
    var t = get_unix_time()
    var j = 0
    var x = 0
    var c = 0
    while j < n:
        j = j + 1
        log(str(j))
    return get_unix_time() - t

sleep(1)

func b_concatlog(n, s):
    sleep(s)
    var t = get_unix_time()
    var j = 0
    var x = 0
    var c = 0
    while j < n:
        j = j + 1
        log("x" + str(j))
    return get_unix_time() - t

sleep(1)

var MODE = get_param("mode", "arith")
var N = int(get_param("n", "6"))
var STEP = int(get_param("step", "3"))
var RUNGS = int(get_param("rungs", "10"))
var S = int(get_param("settle", "3"))
sleep(1)
var k = 0
var r = 0
var el = 0
var cal = 0
var pred = 0
log("KILLBOUND mode=" + MODE + " n=" + str(N) + " step=" + str(STEP) + " rungs=" + str(RUNGS) + " settle=" + str(S) + " entry_cpu=" + str(c0))

cal = b_arith(8, S)
sleep(S)
if cal > 0:
    pred = round(40 / cal)
log("CALIB arith8=" + str(cal) + "s pred_n=" + str(pred) + " (0 = clock too coarse)")

k = N
while r < RUNGS:
    log("RUNG r=" + str(r) + " mode=" + MODE + " n=" + str(k))
    if MODE == "arith":
        el = b_arith(k, S)
    elif MODE == "zero":
        el = b_zero(k, S)
    elif MODE == "cpu":
        el = b_cpu(k, S)
    elif MODE == "time":
        el = b_time(k, S)
    elif MODE == "intlog":
        el = b_intlog(k, S)
    elif MODE == "concatlog":
        el = b_concatlog(k, S)
    else:
        el = b_arith(k, S)
    sleep(S)
    log("OK r=" + str(r) + " n=" + str(k) + " burst=" + str(el) + "s")
    k = k + STEP
    r = r + 1

log("DONE mode=" + MODE + " top_n=" + str(k - STEP) + " nothing died - raise n or step and re-run")
